ref 17592186045417] [17592186157370 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "c834972a-1ed0-585c-803c-906fcea8cea8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157534 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186158250 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157534 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186158251 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157534 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186158252 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157534 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157534 #edb/ref 1045 "Variance of Continuous Random Variables" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157534 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186158250 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157534 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049786 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157534 #edb/ref 1050 840.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157534 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "ecfe7472-c21f-5cb6-9a9a-8377a10cac54" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158250 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186158251 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158250 #edb/ref 1061 29831 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158250 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e48a5625-3306-5288-bda8-33105998d690" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158250 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186158539 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158251 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186158252 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158251 #edb/ref 1061 29832 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158251 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4892229b-e20c-58a7-a09b-56a62bb1e09e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158251 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186157369 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158252 #edb/ref 1061 29834 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158252 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "891a6678-f4fa-595a-b179-6c1d5207f1e4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158252 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186157370 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158539 #edb/ref 1064 "The **variance** of a continuous random variable is the expected squared difference between the variable and its expected value.\n\nFor a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ the variance is defined as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[(X-\\text{E}[X])^{2}] \\\\  & ={\\int }_{S}(x-\\text{E}[X])^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nIn practice, though, it's easier to use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\textrm E [X^2] - \\textrm E[X]^2\n$$\n\n \n\nFor example, consider the continuous random variable $X$ with the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{x}{4}, \\quad 1 < x < 3\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute $\\textrm E[X]{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}x\\cdot \\frac{x}{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{{x}^{3}}{12}|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, we compute $\\textrm E[X^2]{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{x}{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{{x}^{3}}{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{{x}^{4}}{16}|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the variance is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =5-{(\\frac{13}{6})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{36}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158539 #edb/ref 1065 4805 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158539 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "24436a92-f030-567a-aa1c-938371716e3b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158539 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186430366 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{7}{16}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186430366 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186430366 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1204147e-9d6e-5e43-8474-be634d7db4f2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186430367 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{13}{36}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186430367 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186430367 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1efa5566-7842-5b7d-abc6-3035b571fbef" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186430370 #e